Given that for every pair of points \(A\) and \(B\), the line integral \[\int_C (F_1\,dx+F_2\,dy+F_3\,dz)\] is independent of path, the vector field is conservative. Define \[\vec{F}=F_1\hat{i}+F_2\hat{j}+F_3\hat{k}\] For a conservative vector field, \[\vec{F}=\nabla f\] for some scalar potential function \(f(x,y,z)\). Also, \[\nabla\times \vec{F}=0\] Checking the options one by one: <strong>Option A:</strong><br/>For any conservative field, line integral over a closed path is zero: \[\oint_\Gamma \vec{F}\cdot d\vec{r}=0\] Hence, \[\oint_\Gamma (F_1\,dx+F_2\,dy+F_3\,dz)=0\] Therefore, option A is correct. <strong>Option B:</strong><br/>Since the field is conservative, there exists a scalar potential function \(f(x,y,z)\) such that \[F_1=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x},
\qquad
F_2=\frac{\partial f}{\partial y},
\qquad
F_3=\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\] Therefore, option B is correct. <strong>Option C:</strong><br/>Option C states: \[\frac{\partial F_1}{\partial x}
+
\frac{\partial F_2}{\partial y}
+
\frac{\partial F_3}{\partial z}
=0\] This is the condition \[\nabla\cdot \vec{F}=0\] A conservative field need not have zero divergence. Therefore, option C is incorrect. <strong>Option D:</strong><br/>Since \[\nabla\times \vec{F}=0\] we get \[\frac{\partial F_3}{\partial y}
=
\frac{\partial F_2}{\partial z},
\qquad
\frac{\partial F_1}{\partial z}
=
\frac{\partial F_3}{\partial x},
\qquad
\frac{\partial F_2}{\partial x}
=
\frac{\partial F_1}{\partial y}\] Therefore, option D is correct. \[\boxed{A,B,D}\]

