For maximum power transfer, \(R_L=R_{th}\) Deactivate the independent \(50~\text{V}\) source by replacing it with a short circuit and apply a test voltage \(V\) across the load terminals. <img src="img/dbd9c20609f6844b.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,218px)"> Let the current drawn from the test source be \(I\). Applying KCL at the load terminal, \[I
=
\frac{V-V_x}{2}
+
\frac{V}{5}\] \[\because V_x
=
\frac{3}{2+3}V
=
\frac{3V}{5}\] \[\Rightarrow I
=
\frac{V-\dfrac{3V}{5}}{2}
+
\frac{V}{5}=
\frac{V}{5}
+
\frac{V}{5}
=
\frac{2V}{5}\] Hence, \[R_{th}
=
\frac{V}{I}
=
\frac{V}{\dfrac{2V}{5}}
=
\frac{5}{2}
=
2.5~\Omega\] Therefore, for maximum power transfer, \[R_L
=
R_{th}
=
2.5~\Omega\] Hence, \[\boxed{2.5~\Omega}\]

First, compute the oxide capacitance per unit area: \[C_{ox} = \frac{\varepsilon_{ox}}{t_{ox}} = \frac{4 \times 8.85 \times 10^{-14}}{100 \times 10^{-9} \times 100}
    = 35.4 \times 10^{-9}\,\text{F/cm}^2\] The flat-band voltage condition gives: \[V_{FB} = \frac{Q_{ox}}{C_{ox}} + \phi_{ms} = 0\] Since \(Q_{ox}\) is positive, corresponding negative voltage must be applied at gate to achieve flatband condition. Thus \(Q_{ox}/C_{ox}\) is negative: \[\frac{Q_{ox}}{C_{ox}} = \frac{10^{-8}}{35.4 \times 10^{-9}} = \frac{10}{35.4}\,\text{V} \quad \rightarrow \quad -\frac{10}{35.4}\,\text{V (negative contribution)}\] Substituting into the flat-band equation: \[0 = -\frac{10}{35.4} + \phi_{ms}
        \implies
        \phi_{ms} = \frac{10}{35.4}\] Using \(\phi_{ms} = \phi_m - \phi_s\): \[\phi_s = \phi_m - \frac{10}{35.4} = 4.6 - \frac{10}{35.4} = 4.317\,\text{V}\] Therefore, the work function energy of the semiconductor: \[\phi_s = 4.317 \approx 4.32\,\text{eV}\] \[\boxed{4.32}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The metal--semiconductor work function difference is \(\phi_{ms} = \phi_m - \phi_s\); knowing \(\phi_m\) and \(\phi_{ms}\) uniquely determines \(\phi_s\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not confuse the sign of \(Q_{ox}/C_{ox}\): positive oxide charge produces a negative contribution to \(V_{FB}\).</li><li>Ensure \(t_{ox}\) is converted consistently --- mixing nm and cm without conversion is a frequent unit error.</li></ul></div>

