questions.question:
Consider a system \(S\) represented in state space as \[\frac{dx}{dt} =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; -2 \\
1 &amp; -3
\end{bmatrix}
x +
\begin{bmatrix}
1 \\
0
\end{bmatrix}
r,\quad
y =
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp; -5
\end{bmatrix}
x.\] Which of the state space representations given below has/have the same transfer function as that of \(S\)?

questions.explanation:
Given system \(S\) is, \[\dot{x}=Ax+Br\] \[y=Cx\] where, \[A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 &amp; -2\\
1 &amp; -3
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
B=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
C=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
2 &amp; -5
\end{array}\right]\] Transfer function is given by, \[G(s)=C(sI-A)^{-1}B\] First compute \(sI-A\), \[sI-A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s &amp; 2\\
-1 &amp; s+3
\end{array}\right]\] Determinant is, \[|sI-A|
=
s(s+3)-2(-1)\] \[=s^2+3s+2\] \[=(s+1)(s+2)\] Therefore, \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+3 &amp; -2\\
1 &amp; s
\end{array}\right]\] Now multiply with \(B\), \[(sI-A)^{-1}B
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+3 &amp; -2\\
1 &amp; s
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
s+3\\
1
\end{array}\right]\] Multiplying with \(C\), \[G(s)
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
2 &amp; -5
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
s+3\\
1
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{2(s+3)-5}{s^2+3s+2}\] \[=
\frac{2s+1}{s^2+3s+2}\] Now we check each option individually. <strong>Option A</strong> \[A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 &amp; 1\\
-2 &amp; -3
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
B=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
C=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 &amp; 2
\end{array}\right]\] Compute transfer function. \[sI-A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s &amp; -1\\
2 &amp; s+3
\end{array}\right]\] Determinant is, \[s(s+3)-(-1)(2)
=
s^2+3s+2\] \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+3 &amp; 1\\
-2 &amp; s
\end{array}\right]\] \[(sI-A)^{-1}B
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
s
\end{array}\right]\] \[G_{A}(s)
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 &amp; 2
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
s
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{2s+1}{s^2+3s+2}\] Hence, option A has same transfer function. <strong>Option B</strong> \[A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 &amp; 1\\
-2 &amp; -3
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
B=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
C=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 &amp; 2
\end{array}\right]\] Using previously calculated inverse matrix, \[(sI-A)^{-1}B
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
s+3\\
-2
\end{array}\right]\] \[G_{B}(s)
=
\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 &amp; 2
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
s+3\\
-2
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{-4}{s^2+3s+2}\] This is not equal to original transfer function. Hence, option B is false. <strong>Option C</strong> \[A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
B=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
-1\\
3
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
C=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 &amp; 1
\end{array}\right]\] Since \(A\) is diagonal, \[sI-A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; s+2
\end{array}\right]\] \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\dfrac{1}{s+1} &amp; 0\\ 0 &amp; \dfrac{1}{s+2}
\end{array}\right]\] \[(sI-A)^{-1}B
=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
\dfrac{-1}{s+1}\\ \dfrac{3}{s+2}
\end{array}\right]\] \[G_{C}(s)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 &amp; 1
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
\dfrac{-1}{s+1}\\ \dfrac{3}{s+2}
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{-1}{s+1}
+
\frac{3}{s+2}\] \[=
\frac{-(s+2)+3(s+1)}
{(s+1)(s+2)}\] \[=
\frac{2s+1}{s^2+3s+2}\] Hence, option C has same transfer function. <strong>Option D</strong> \[A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
B=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
C=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 &amp; 2
\end{array}\right]\] \[(sI-A)^{-1}B
=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
\dfrac{1}{s+1}\\ \dfrac{1}{s+2}
\end{array}\right]\] \[G_{D}(s)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 &amp; 2
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
\dfrac{1}{s+1}\\ \dfrac{1}{s+2}
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{1}{s+1}
+
\frac{2}{s+2}\] \[=
\frac{(s+2)+2(s+1)}
{(s+1)(s+2)}\] \[=
\frac{3s+4}
{s^2+3s+2}\] This is not equal to original transfer function. Hence, option D is false. \[\boxed{
\text{(A) }\dfrac{dx}{dt}=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 1\\
-2 &amp; -3
\end{bmatrix}x+
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}r,\;
y=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix}x
}\] \[\boxed{
\text{(C) }\dfrac{dx}{dt}=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix}x+
\begin{bmatrix}
-1\\
3
\end{bmatrix}r,\;
y=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}x
}\]

